00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:17.180
So few things to think about.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:20.270
Why is machine learning and
AI a big deal now, right?

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:25.730
It's been around for 30
years, used by space, defense,

00:05:25.730 --> 00:05:28.190
and all kinds of security
industries, and so on.

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:30.070
But what's the inflection point?

00:05:30.070 --> 00:05:32.750
The inflection point,
really, is a combination

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:38.270
of cloud computing, mobile, for
us the cost of processing going

00:05:38.270 --> 00:05:40.790
down, which means you
can run a lot more

00:05:40.790 --> 00:05:45.140
mathematical computation now
to make simple decisions.

00:05:45.140 --> 00:05:49.992
The decisions could be around
a classification problem--

00:05:49.992 --> 00:05:51.950
you're trying to classify
whether the filter is

00:05:51.950 --> 00:05:54.280
good or bad, which is
an example we look at--

00:05:54.280 --> 00:05:57.160
or regression, which is
what I did for 17 years.

00:05:57.160 --> 00:05:59.660
You know, what's the demand
forecast for the next 12

00:05:59.660 --> 00:06:01.344
months, 24 months, and so on?

00:06:01.344 --> 00:06:03.260
And we we used all kinds
of statistical models

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:06.610
and got an accuracy of 70%.

00:06:06.610 --> 00:06:10.330
Now, when we went into
one of these companies,

00:06:10.330 --> 00:06:13.600
and when we said, hey, we
are going to video whatever

00:06:13.600 --> 00:06:15.080
the best model you have--

00:06:15.080 --> 00:06:18.100
we have statistical
model softwares

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:19.802
and so on, there
are like, we have

00:06:19.802 --> 00:06:21.010
been doing this for 20 years.

00:06:21.010 --> 00:06:23.680
There's no way you come in
for six weeks and beat that.

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:26.190
To cut the long story
short, we beat it by 20%,

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:28.720
and it was a bit shocking
and disturbing to them

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:31.720
that we challenged some of
their best demand planners.

00:06:31.720 --> 00:06:32.470
Right?

00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:34.780
Now the point I'm
trying to make here

00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:37.390
is, this is a paradigm shift.

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:39.280
And why there's a
paradigm shift is

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:43.180
because we are taking something
very fundamental to us

00:06:43.180 --> 00:06:47.680
and allowing a machine to do
that, which is decision making.

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:50.040
If you look at an
enterprise, look

00:06:50.040 --> 00:06:52.530
at whether it's invoice
processing, to supply ordering,

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:54.980
to demand planning,
to replacing a part,

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:57.630
maintenance-- there
are small decisions

00:06:57.630 --> 00:07:01.470
made at every stage, many
decisions made by a human.

00:07:01.470 --> 00:07:03.600
Some decisions are
very expensive.

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:09.000
If you make a bad decision,
it can cost a lot of money

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:10.830
as well as a lot of disruption.

00:07:10.830 --> 00:07:12.810
Now you're taking that
very simple decision

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:16.020
making and making that into a
machine learning model, which

00:07:16.020 --> 00:07:21.120
you'll host on GCP, which is a
very quick demo I'll show you

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:23.150
as well.

00:07:23.150 --> 00:07:27.850
So in this journey when we take,
whether it's filter replacement

00:07:27.850 --> 00:07:31.250
or forecasting, if you
leave it up to humans--

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:32.800
and by the way,
this is by no means

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:35.920
to say that we will
replace humans.

00:07:35.920 --> 00:07:38.230
It's to augment humans--

00:07:38.230 --> 00:07:40.870
to improve the accuracy
level, to help,

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:44.380
to make better decisions, to
do the filter replacement,

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:47.740
to watch filter replacement,
not waking up and checking

00:07:47.740 --> 00:07:49.810
the filters and the
beer quality every time,

00:07:49.810 --> 00:07:54.460
but letting the machine do that
day in and day out 24 by 7.

00:07:54.460 --> 00:07:59.110
So leaving it up to humans,
in this decision making there

00:07:59.110 --> 00:08:01.420
is bias involved.

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:04.750
We call it experience,
to some extent.

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:07.670
But by the time we get
decades of experience,

00:08:07.670 --> 00:08:10.270
there's a bias on what
data columns are important,

00:08:10.270 --> 00:08:11.950
what's not important.

00:08:11.950 --> 00:08:15.220
We ignore some of the
signals in the data patterns

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:17.310
on the columns we ignored.

00:08:17.310 --> 00:08:20.200
Like, in some of the
larger enterprises,

00:08:20.200 --> 00:08:23.190
we looked at 200
columns of data.

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:26.110
Humans wouldn't practically have
time and energy and capacity

00:08:26.110 --> 00:08:30.360
to scan through that
every hour 24 by 7,

00:08:30.360 --> 00:08:34.059
and look at patterns
across those 200 columns

00:08:34.059 --> 00:08:36.130
continuously being generated.

00:08:36.130 --> 00:08:40.289
So that's where machine
learning plays a role,

00:08:40.289 --> 00:08:42.280
and some of the
slides that I'm going

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:44.970
to share before we go
into the brewing example

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:49.440
is to have you understand
that the conversations

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:51.930
that we are having, and the
experimentation and innovation

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:55.560
that's happening is not just
about predictive maintenance,

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:58.110
but in the whole
supply chain ecosystem.

00:08:58.110 --> 00:09:02.860
We get involved with how
machine learning and AI can help

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:05.620
at beating demand
forecast-- in this case,

00:09:05.620 --> 00:09:08.600
the example I gave for
enterprise, beating by 20%--

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:12.910
or reducing supply inventory
carrying costs by 50%.

00:09:12.910 --> 00:09:15.420
There's a publicly-posted
case study

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:18.870
on Google Cloud on how we
improved [INAUDIBLE] for Amazon

00:09:18.870 --> 00:09:21.530
seller by 50%.

00:09:21.530 --> 00:09:24.270
Their inventory carrying
cost went down by 50%.

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:28.950
So similarly, like, can
the deliveries be improved?

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:31.620
What opportunities did
I miss because I did not

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:33.750
understand my customer better?

00:09:33.750 --> 00:09:37.050
When I say customer engagement,
customer experience management,

00:09:37.050 --> 00:09:40.020
it's seen as a marketing
and a sales problem,

00:09:40.020 --> 00:09:43.010
but you are selling a product.

00:09:43.010 --> 00:09:46.500
In the e-tailers or
online retailers world,

00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:49.020
the customers are
looking on a website,

00:09:49.020 --> 00:09:51.720
looking at your email
campaigning, placing an order.

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:54.920
You do not want to run
in a no stock situation.

00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:57.650
Or even if you have
stock, you do not

00:09:57.650 --> 00:10:01.610
want price to be the factor
where the customer doesn't buy.

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:04.370
Or you do not want it to
be a quality issue which

00:10:04.370 --> 00:10:08.120
is a factor which is reflected
in the customer comments,

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:09.750
sentiments, and so on.

00:10:09.750 --> 00:10:13.220
So these are this variety
of problems that we

00:10:13.220 --> 00:10:16.230
are solving for our customers.

00:10:16.230 --> 00:10:20.420
Now, when it comes to preventive
maintenance and so on-- so

00:10:20.420 --> 00:10:25.220
whether it's tracking inventory,
defective inventory, logistics,

00:10:25.220 --> 00:10:29.870
or equipment failing, these
are all different scenarios

00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:35.420
where humans are applying
their decision making.

00:10:35.420 --> 00:10:39.540
Again, there are certain
decisions which are simple,

00:10:39.540 --> 00:10:43.680
or we can make it a component,
as in, this particular decision

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:47.040
I can build an ML model,
and this one I cannot.

00:10:47.040 --> 00:10:49.470
And realizing that
is a very key element

00:10:49.470 --> 00:10:52.080
in this innovation exercise.

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:55.890
So Adam, would you like to add
