distribution impact.

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:03.720
So something as simple
as one part replacement

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:06.880
can disrupt the
whole supply chain.

00:13:06.880 --> 00:13:12.060
So when companies-- whether
it's AB InBev or some large

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:13.420
enterprises--

00:13:13.420 --> 00:13:16.930
when they look at automation,
looking and talking to Google--

00:13:16.930 --> 00:13:19.570
and fortunately I've been
involved in many of these

00:13:19.570 --> 00:13:22.750
factory automations of global
companies who are distributed

00:13:22.750 --> 00:13:24.340
around the world--

00:13:24.340 --> 00:13:26.620
with the C-level
executives, they

00:13:26.620 --> 00:13:30.670
think about the entire factory
automation, all the way

00:13:30.670 --> 00:13:35.110
from the time a raw material
is brought into the door

00:13:35.110 --> 00:13:37.930
to a time a finished good
leaves the door, right?

00:13:37.930 --> 00:13:41.540
What all can I automate
using machine learning, AI,

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:44.560
IoT sensors, and the fact
that we are collecting

00:13:44.560 --> 00:13:46.340
all the data on the cloud.

00:13:46.340 --> 00:13:48.460
Now what can I automate?

00:13:48.460 --> 00:13:51.960
Because now the expectation of
delivery lead time is reduced,

00:13:51.960 --> 00:13:56.470
and expectation of downtimes
reduction is increasing.

00:13:56.470 --> 00:13:58.990
So there is a lot of these
things happening at the same

00:13:58.990 --> 00:14:02.040
time, because the C-level
executives-- the CTOs,

00:14:02.040 --> 00:14:02.770
the CIOs--

00:14:02.770 --> 00:14:05.200
are realizing now
this is a power

00:14:05.200 --> 00:14:06.730
that they need to harness.

00:14:06.730 --> 00:14:09.580
So AB InBev, through
their incubator program

00:14:09.580 --> 00:14:14.110
that Adam just
mentioned, has been

00:14:14.110 --> 00:14:16.370
thinking about that
for more than a year,

00:14:16.370 --> 00:14:18.040
or a year and a half.

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:21.200
And these are companies
that are, like many,

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:22.450
many decades old.

00:14:22.450 --> 00:14:26.410
And for them to be looking at
this cloud and machine learning

00:14:26.410 --> 00:14:30.040
and saying, where can I
implement machine learning

00:14:30.040 --> 00:14:33.670
and AI to improve my
overall manufacturing

00:14:33.670 --> 00:14:37.750
experience, and so
on, is something that,

00:14:37.750 --> 00:14:41.330
whether it's AB InBev or
similar large companies,

00:14:41.330 --> 00:14:44.570
we are looking at where we
plug in which component.

00:14:44.570 --> 00:14:47.990
Can I improve the
safety of manufacturing?

00:14:47.990 --> 00:14:50.420
Can we decrease the
production downtime?

00:14:50.420 --> 00:14:53.360
Can we improve the
quality of the product?

00:14:53.360 --> 00:14:55.610
So some of you have that.

00:14:55.610 --> 00:15:00.215
In the keynote today
by Fei-Fei Lee,

00:15:00.215 --> 00:15:04.160
I believe she
presented on auto ML,

00:15:04.160 --> 00:15:06.200
and it's pretty fascinating.

00:15:06.200 --> 00:15:09.260
We are working with a few
customers on some pretty

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:12.320
innovative use cases.

00:15:12.320 --> 00:15:17.662
So auto ML is like a hybrid
where you don't really

00:15:17.662 --> 00:15:19.870
need to get into the depth
of machine learning model,

00:15:19.870 --> 00:15:24.190
yet with your dataset you
can get prediction results.

00:15:24.190 --> 00:15:26.600
And then there are two
extremes to the left and right,

00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:29.930
pre-built ML model models, which
are Vision API or Translation

00:15:29.930 --> 00:15:30.525
API.

00:15:30.525 --> 00:15:31.900
These are machine
learning models

00:15:31.900 --> 00:15:36.560
that Google is
continuously training.

00:15:36.560 --> 00:15:39.190
All you need to pass is an
input, and you get a result.

00:15:39.190 --> 00:15:41.610
Then on the right side
is the customer ML model,

00:15:41.610 --> 00:15:45.480
which is what I'm going to demo,
which is more sophisticated,

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:47.130
which gives you a
lot of flexibility,

00:15:47.130 --> 00:15:50.700
but you have to build your
own machine learning model.

00:15:50.700 --> 00:15:54.027
So when you look
at this process,

00:15:54.027 --> 00:15:55.610
you don't want to
look at it as, where

00:15:55.610 --> 00:16:00.020
can I use this fancy or
shiny object called machine

00:16:00.020 --> 00:16:02.610
learning AI, which is what many
customers sometimes start off

00:16:02.610 --> 00:16:04.360
to think, that OK,
there's something cool.

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:06.080
I can use this, and so on.

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:09.110
Because at the end of the
day, if you don't really

00:16:09.110 --> 00:16:11.970
tie to the use case, if
you don't tie to the KPIs,

00:16:11.970 --> 00:16:15.410
and if you don't convince
somebody like Adam,

00:16:15.410 --> 00:16:17.390
this project doesn't
go too far, right?

00:16:17.390 --> 00:16:20.300
And then he has to, in turn,
align with the stakeholders

00:16:20.300 --> 00:16:21.360
and so on.

00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:23.420
So you have to relate
to the business

00:16:23.420 --> 00:16:25.400
and how we are
going to transform.

00:16:25.400 --> 00:16:27.620
20, 25 years back,
if somebody said

00:16:27.620 --> 00:16:30.750
internet will change many
aspects of your business,

00:16:30.750 --> 00:16:34.070
and every department, it would
have been hard for many people

00:16:34.070 --> 00:16:35.540
to comprehend.

00:16:35.540 --> 00:16:41.660
We are dealing with the same
thing, and some leaders claim--

00:16:41.660 --> 00:16:43.555
which I believe it's true--

00:16:43.555 --> 00:16:44.930
that this is going
to be a bigger

00:16:44.930 --> 00:16:47.690
revolution than internet era.

00:16:47.690 --> 00:16:48.650
Why?

00:16:48.650 --> 00:16:52.010
Because we are giving our
decision-making aspect

00:16:52.010 --> 00:16:53.930
and taking help of a machine.

00:16:53.930 --> 00:16:56.700
That's it's quite
hard for humans to do.

00:16:56.700 --> 00:16:58.580
But once you do
that, and you know

00:16:58.580 --> 00:17:00.740
that it's incredibly
powerful, now we

00:17:00.740 --> 00:17:03.590
get to the next level with that.

00:17:03.590 --> 00:17:07.700
We're going to obviously talk
about the brewing example--

00:17:07.700 --> 00:17:09.540
what we did and so on.

00:17:09.540 --> 00:17:12.210
So I spoke to you about
a few other examples.

00:17:12.210 --> 00:17:15.650
So now the customers are looking
at it from a 360-degree angle,

00:17:15.650 --> 00:17:16.369
right?

00:17:16.369 --> 00:17:19.760
What can I do for my
preventive maintenance,

00:17:19.760 --> 00:17:21.897
my demand, my supply?

00:17:21.897 --> 00:17:23.480
If I can control my
demand and supply,

00:17:23.480 --> 00:17:25.339
can also I adjust my price?

00:17:25.339 --> 00:17:27.710
Think about an
online retailer who

00:17:27.710 --> 00:17:31.190
has to make decisions and
price points every hour

00:17:31.190 --> 00:17:34.370
or every few hours based
on stock, based on demand,

00:17:34.370 --> 00:17:35.340
and so on.

00:17:35.340 --> 00:17:38.316
So for them pricing is
not a separate department,

00:17:38.316 --> 00:17:39.940
marketing is not a
separate department,

00:17:39.940 --> 00:17:42.590
and supply demand planning
is not a separate department.

00:17:42.590 --> 00:17:44.860
Even if they exist as
a separate department,

00:17:44.860 --> 00:17:48.810
they work in a secular,
harmonious way.

00:17:48.810 --> 00:17:54.310
So with that, now the
customers are looking at,

00:17:54.310 --> 00:17:58.450
can you automate different
machineries, devices, X-ray

00:17:58.450 --> 00:17:59.020
machines.

00:17:59.020 --> 00:18:03.317
Can you tell me ahead of time
when the machine will go down?

00:18:03.317 --> 00:18:04.650
That's the most important thing.

00:18:04.650 --> 00:18:09.700
If you tell right when it fails,
or just a day before it fails,

00:18:09.700 --> 00:18:10.610
maybe it's useful.

00:18:10.610 --> 00:18:13.480
But if you can predict a month
or two months in advance,

00:18:13.480 --> 00:18:18.550
they can line up servicing
folks to show up at the factory.

00:18:18.550 --> 00:18:22.720
They can have downtime planned
during a Sunday morning

00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:24.230
when nobody's working.

00:18:24.230 --> 00:18:27.130
So these are things
that we are looking at.

00:18:27.130 --> 00:18:31.960
You know, how you take existing
data in the ERPs or wherever

00:18:31.960 --> 00:18:38.390
and apply GCP machine learning,
and reduce the risk level,

00:18:38.390 --> 00:18:43.270
improve fault detection, and
have more accurate replacement

00:18:43.270 --> 00:18:45.490
dates, and so on.

00:18:45.490 --> 00:18:51.720
So going a little deeper into
the beer exercise, itself.

00:18:51.720 --> 00:18:54.060
It all started
with, what can we do

00:18:54.060 --> 00:18:56.380
with machine learning and AI.

00:18:56.380 --> 00:18:59.370
So this is our
business, and then

00:18:59.370 --> 00:19:01.620
figure out what you can do.

00:19:01.620 --> 00:19:04.020
So we had an option to
pick 16 different ideas.

00:19:04.020 --> 00:19:07.040
We picked filter replacement.

00:19:07.040 --> 00:19:08.330
Why?

00:19:08.330 --> 00:19:10.340
Because it had a lot of data.

00:19:10.340 --> 00:19:12.980
With machine learning-- if
you've not heard of this

00:19:12.980 --> 00:19:14.120
before--

00:19:14.120 --> 00:19:17.420
without data you don't have
a machine learning model.

00:19:17.420 --> 00:19:19.310
Without enough data, I mean.

00:19:19.310 --> 00:19:21.460
Number one.

00:19:21.460 --> 00:19:24.030
Number two is, you have
to bring in a little bit

00:19:24.030 --> 00:19:27.690
of your innovation thinking
hat, because there's

00:19:27.690 --> 00:19:30.600
a bit of paradigm shift in
designing these models, right?

00:19:30.600 --> 00:19:34.556
And also you have
to think about how

00:19:34.556 --> 00:19:37.950
do you harness information what
you have not harnessed before?

00:19:37.950 --> 00:19:39.750
Data exists in many
forms, and we'll

00:19:39.750 --> 00:19:42.860
talk about that in a minute.

00:19:42.860 --> 00:19:50.800
So when we talk about this
filter replacement use case,

00:19:50.800 --> 00:19:53.990
we validated that we
have a lot of data.

00:19:53.990 --> 00:19:58.270
And we understood the
business process behind it.

00:19:58.270 --> 00:20:00.160
If we replace this
filter, what's

00:20:00.160 --> 00:20:02.620
the business benefit,
how much does it save,

00:20:02.620 --> 00:20:04.760
what's the business
impact, and so on.

00:20:04.760 --> 00:20:09.580
So the business rationale
to do this was justifiable.

00:20:09.580 --> 00:20:14.380
Now beyond that, not only
were we going to save money,

00:20:14.380 --> 00:20:18.180
but we had a chance to
improve the taste of the beer.

00:20:18.180 --> 00:20:21.440
And with that started
this whole journey

00:20:21.440 --> 00:20:26.050
of figuring out what can
be harnessed with data.

00:20:26.050 --> 00:20:27.310
And there are lots of data.

00:20:27.310 --> 00:20:31.440
When I say lots of
data in their ERP,

00:20:31.440 --> 00:20:35.490
it's almost like 200 columns
of many different aspects

00:20:35.490 --> 00:20:39.190
of the whole brewing
and production process.

00:20:39.190 --> 00:20:42.010
Now we have to look at all
these different sets of columns

00:20:42.010 --> 00:20:42.510
of data.

00:20:42.510 --> 00:20:44.259
Think of it as a massive
Excel spreadsheet

00:20:44.259 --> 00:20:46.065
for the sake of simplicity.

00:20:46.065 --> 00:20:50.520
A massive amount of information
coming in continuously,

00:20:50.520 --> 00:20:53.340
and a brewmaster
relies on few columns,

00:20:53.340 --> 00:20:56.550
because that's what he has
relied for a long time,

00:20:56.550 --> 00:21:01.320
for decades, and for the
most part he's been right.

00:21:01.320 --> 00:21:03.120
Now we have all this
data, and we have

00:21:03.120 --> 00:21:05.200
to improve the accuracy level.

00:21:05.200 --> 00:21:07.050
So when we looked at--

00:21:07.050 --> 00:21:09.120
before we actually
rolled out the model,

00:21:09.120 --> 00:21:13.600
the accuracy level was at
60% to 70% of the best.

00:21:13.600 --> 00:21:16.760
This is a brewmaster
looking at the beer

00:21:16.760 --> 00:21:20.490
as it comes out of the kettle,
passes through the filter,

00:21:20.490 --> 00:21:24.270
and checking certain gauges,
and then also looking

00:21:24.270 --> 00:21:26.310
at the beer quality itself.

00:21:26.310 --> 00:21:27.940
They are the
particles in the beer,

00:21:27.940 --> 00:21:30.580
and there are terms like
turbidity, and so on.

00:21:30.580 --> 00:21:33.270
So he gauges that, and he
determines whether the beer

00:21:33.270 --> 00:21:36.150
is good or not good.

00:21:36.150 --> 00:21:44.160
Now the model has to tell when
the filter has gone wrong--

00:21:44.160 --> 00:21:46.020
not too early, not too late.

00:21:46.020 --> 00:21:49.870
So now we got to a
92% accuracy level.

00:21:49.870 --> 00:21:55.310
Now this is something that
has been not attempted before,

00:21:55.310 --> 00:21:57.240
nor was thought possible.

00:21:57.240 --> 00:22:00.210
So what was even more
brilliant was the fact

00:22:00.210 --> 00:22:04.815
that the brewmaster was very
involved in building this out,

00:22:04.815 --> 00:22:09.160
and he acknowledged that it's
hard for humans to beat this.

00:22:09.160 --> 00:22:11.370
And we were quite amazed
that somebody as experienced

00:22:11.370 --> 00:22:14.580
as that, and he's ready to
go through a transformation

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:15.810
and a change.

00:22:15.810 --> 00:22:18.840
So Adam do you want to add
anything to it as we progress?

00:22:18.840 --> 00:22:19.980
ADAM SPUNBERG: Sure.

00:22:19.980 --> 00:22:22.290
And, you know, the
thing I find interesting

00:22:22.290 --> 00:22:27.030
is we're trying to improve
the quality of the tasting

00:22:27.030 --> 00:22:31.110
of the beer, but I find that
the more beers you drink,

00:22:31.110 --> 00:22:34.630
the better machine learning
and AI seems to taste as well.

00:22:34.630 --> 00:22:38.100
So it's kind of a
compelling counterpoint.

00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:41.010
No, but here's what I'll say is
that, I think a lot of people

00:22:41.010 --> 00:22:43.320
don't know this about
brewing, and I myself actually

00:22:43.320 --> 00:22:45.420
didn't know this
until I really got

00:22:45.420 --> 00:22:48.030
into the fabric of AB InBev.

00:22:48.030 --> 00:22:53.640
Brewing is a really
difficult, long process.

00:22:53.640 --> 00:22:56.610
It's not like you just
create some concoction,

00:22:56.610 --> 00:23:01.050
pour it in a can,
and lock it up.

00:23:01.050 --> 00:23:04.455
In some cases, depending
on what the brand is,

00:23:04.455 --> 00:23:07.620
it can be a month-long
fermentation process.

00:23:07.620 --> 00:23:10.200
It's really almost an art form.

00:23:10.200 --> 00:23:14.030
And so make an analogy
of what you were saying,

00:23:14.030 --> 00:23:15.720
the brewmaster--

00:23:15.720 --> 00:23:17.800
obviously you still need
that human component,

00:23:17.800 --> 00:23:22.020
but it's sort of like in chess
when the computers finally

00:23:22.020 --> 00:23:23.640
started beating humans.

00:23:23.640 --> 00:23:26.490
At some point, no matter
how talented you are,

00:23:26.490 --> 00:23:30.840
you just can't quite be at that
level that a computer can be.

00:23:30.840 --> 00:23:34.560
And so we as a company can
either shy away from that

00:23:34.560 --> 00:23:37.920
or try to find a way to embrace
that in a way that keeps us

00:23:37.920 --> 00:23:42.300
competitive and trying to have
that marketing position that we

00:23:42.300 --> 00:23:44.160
have within the world.

00:23:44.160 --> 00:23:46.980
So if we're not interested
in these things,

00:23:46.980 --> 00:23:50.830
and not exploring what I think
is unmistakably the future--

00:23:50.830 --> 00:23:53.130
and it's not just beer.

00:23:53.130 --> 00:23:54.960
We're using this
as our case study,

00:23:54.960 --> 00:23:57.190
but this could be anything.

00:23:57.190 --> 00:23:59.640
I mean, I think even
beyond the level

00:23:59.640 --> 00:24:02.310
that we can even
comprehend right now.

00:24:02.310 --> 00:24:03.700
This is just the beginning.

00:24:03.700 --> 00:24:06.390
So I don't know if I'm
convinced, personally, that it

00:24:06.390 --> 00:24:09.540
will be a bigger revolution
than the internet,

00:24:09.540 --> 00:24:14.310
but I also think that
100 years from now we

00:24:14.310 --> 00:24:16.110
might be bowing to robots.

00:24:16.110 --> 00:24:17.380
So who knows?

00:24:17.380 --> 00:24:18.990
It's really hard to predict.

00:24:18.990 --> 00:24:22.080
But I think at this stage,
this is a responsible AI.

00:24:22.080 --> 00:24:23.380
This is safe.

00:24:23.380 --> 00:24:27.180
This is controlled settings in
which you're using technology

00:24:27.180 --> 00:24:30.840
to improve production in
a way that helps business,

00:24:30.840 --> 00:24:33.990
and also, we hope,
gives you a chance

00:24:33.990 --> 00:24:36.700
to take that gulp of
Budweiser and say,

00:24:36.700 --> 00:24:39.120
oh, the aftertaste was
just a little bit better

00:24:39.120 --> 00:24:40.890
than last week.

00:24:40.890 --> 00:24:42.390
That's what we're
going for, anyway.

00:24:44.974 --> 00:24:46.890
MANJUNATH DEVADAS: Like
we were talking about,

00:24:46.890 --> 00:24:48.660
the filter replacement itself.

00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:51.980
So it's about looking
at these kettles,

00:24:51.980 --> 00:24:55.140
looking at the filters,
and instead of a human

00:24:55.140 --> 00:24:58.320
judging when to
replace the filter,

00:24:58.320 --> 00:25:03.790
it is using that data
to say the patterns

00:25:03.790 --> 00:25:05.970
of this 200-all columns.

00:25:05.970 --> 00:25:07.750
And you do feature
engineering, and get

00:25:07.750 --> 00:25:12.100
to selecting 15 or 20 columns,
which our data scientists do.

00:25:12.100 --> 00:25:14.590
Say, OK, these are 20
which seems to be relevant.

00:25:14.590 --> 00:25:17.770
Now, again keep, in mind
these data scientists are not

00:25:17.770 --> 00:25:19.930
experts in brewing business.

00:25:19.930 --> 00:25:23.470
We have separate domain experts
who understand supply chain

00:25:23.470 --> 00:25:26.980
and so on, and we have
the customer work with us

00:25:26.980 --> 00:25:28.630
through this innovation.

00:25:28.630 --> 00:25:31.090
And we say, hey, we see a
data pattern which indicates

00:25:31.090 --> 00:25:32.750
that the filter is going wrong.

00:25:32.750 --> 00:25:34.000
It doesn't seem to make sense.

00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:36.670
And the brewmaster and
experts at AB InBev

00:25:36.670 --> 00:25:38.560
say, oh yeah, that
seems to be more

00:25:38.560 --> 00:25:40.080
accurate than how we do it.

00:25:40.080 --> 00:25:43.870
So that's kind of how you
go through it relatively.

00:25:43.870 --> 00:25:46.110
And when you do this,
what's important

00:25:46.110 --> 00:25:50.470
is, as the beer
quality degrades,

00:25:50.470 --> 00:25:53.150
it's not a very definitive
time when the beer goes bad,

00:25:53.150 --> 00:25:56.510
and it kind of
gradually goes bad.

00:25:56.510 --> 00:25:59.680
And ideally you want
to get to a point we're

00:25:59.680 --> 00:26:04.930
you know that it has now crossed
that line where it doesn't meet

00:26:04.930 --> 00:26:05.740
quality control.

00:26:05.740 --> 00:26:09.350
And because it's
a drink, and there

00:26:09.350 --> 00:26:11.290
is subjectivity to
taste and clarity,

00:26:11.290 --> 00:26:13.600
and a few other factors
of quality control,

00:26:13.600 --> 00:26:16.020
you have to kind of pick
it at the right time.

00:26:16.020 --> 00:26:19.450
You pick it too
slow or too late,

00:26:19.450 --> 00:26:21.960
then you've let go,
you know, you've

00:26:21.960 --> 00:26:24.040
rolled out a whole bunch
of lower quality beer.

00:26:31.530 --> 00:26:34.790
If you replace it too
early, you've not only

00:26:34.790 --> 00:26:36.660
wasted a whole
bunch of good beer,

00:26:36.660 --> 00:26:39.850
but also you've increased the
cost of filter replacement--

00:26:39.850 --> 00:26:42.000
more filter replacements.

00:26:42.000 --> 00:26:45.950
So getting it right
at the right time

00:26:45.950 --> 00:26:47.490
was the most important thing.

00:26:47.490 --> 00:26:53.700
And moving away
from 60% accuracy,

00:26:53.700 --> 00:26:57.920
and avoiding the situation
where there is 40% inaccuracy.

00:26:57.920 --> 00:27:00.610
When I say inaccuracy, as
in you replace the filter

00:27:00.610 --> 00:27:04.880
when you should have not, or
you did not replace the filter

00:27:04.880 --> 00:27:07.460
when you should have.

00:27:07.460 --> 00:27:11.000
And a lot of the inaccuracies
was because of the fact--

00:27:11.000 --> 00:27:12.500
and this happens
in many enterprises

00:27:12.500 --> 00:27:15.650
in many different ways in
other use cases as well.

00:27:15.650 --> 00:27:19.850
Because of our experience,
we think certain attributes

00:27:19.850 --> 00:27:20.915
are most relevant.

00:27:20.915 --> 00:27:23.540
It could be clarity of beer, it
could be temperature, pressure,

00:27:23.540 --> 00:27:25.084
it could be something, right?

00:27:25.084 --> 00:27:27.500
Basically if I'm the brewmaster,
if I had a light on this,

00:27:27.500 --> 00:27:30.040
I'm going to look for these
data, and whenever it exceeds,

00:27:30.040 --> 00:27:32.210
beer looks a certain
color, then I'm

00:27:32.210 --> 00:27:34.580
going to decide that the
filter is ready to be replaced,

00:27:34.580 --> 00:27:36.320
and I do that.

00:27:36.320 --> 00:27:39.230
But there are a
lot of data which

00:27:39.230 --> 00:27:44.990
don't have very obvious
signals or signs, which it's

00:27:44.990 --> 00:27:49.810
hard for us to apply that,
because when you have

00:27:49.810 --> 00:27:52.270
hard data continuously getting
updated, and 200 columns,

00:27:52.270 --> 00:27:53.728
and so on, it's
kind of hard for us

00:27:53.728 --> 00:27:57.490
to look and see the
significance of this other data.

00:27:57.490 --> 00:28:00.760
This is where, when you
apply machine learning model,

00:28:00.760 --> 00:28:04.990
you are now looking at not only
data that you always looked at,

00:28:04.990 --> 00:28:07.900
but also data which you
thought was less significant.

00:28:07.900 --> 00:28:10.390
This is where our data
scientists go into feature

00:28:10.390 --> 00:28:12.880
engineering, and we look
at all the columns and say,

00:28:12.880 --> 00:28:14.920
these original columns
seem to be significant,

00:28:14.920 --> 00:28:18.520
because when the data
arrives at this pattern, then

00:28:18.520 --> 00:28:20.860
it's indicating that
the machine learning--

00:28:20.860 --> 00:28:24.100
then it's indicating that it's
time to replace the filter.

00:28:24.100 --> 00:28:27.040
So with that now what
you're really doing

00:28:27.040 --> 00:28:29.455
is you're understanding
your process,

00:28:29.455 --> 00:28:31.900
you're understanding
the parameters of data

00:28:31.900 --> 00:28:35.860
that you capture, and build
the machine learning model.

00:28:35.860 --> 00:28:37.520
Before I go into
the model itself,

00:28:37.520 --> 00:28:39.170
Adam, do you want
to add anything?

00:28:39.170 --> 00:28:39.920
ADAM SPUNBERG: No.

00:28:39.920 --> 00:28:43.650
I would just say to echo
what you were saying,

00:28:43.650 --> 00:28:50.390
Manju, there is there really
is a precise art to this.

00:28:50.390 --> 00:28:54.510
And to give a really
simple example,

00:28:54.510 --> 00:28:57.530
I don't know if you all remember
those games that, at least,

00:28:57.530 --> 00:29:00.300
we had when I was a kid, where
you'd try to roll a ball,

00:29:00.300 --> 00:29:03.720
and there'd be two humps,
and if you push it too hard

00:29:03.720 --> 00:29:05.100
it would go over
the second hump,

00:29:05.100 --> 00:29:07.980
but if you didn't push it far
enough, it didn't make it.

00:29:07.980 --> 00:29:11.280
You're trying to find that
sweet spot in the middle.

00:29:11.280 --> 00:29:14.310
And because you have to err
on the side of caution--

00:29:14.310 --> 00:29:16.620
this is, after all,
a consumer product

00:29:16.620 --> 00:29:18.360
that people are
drinking-- we're never

00:29:18.360 --> 00:29:20.610
going to release something
into the marketplace that's

00:29:20.610 --> 00:29:21.762
not up to standard.

00:29:21.762 --> 00:29:23.970
We're going to err on the
side of being more careful.

00:29:23.970 --> 00:29:27.840
What this allows us to do
is to know with certainty--

00:29:27.840 --> 00:29:29.700
or with almost near
certainty, and we'll

00:29:29.700 --> 00:29:31.560
keep perfecting that model--

00:29:31.560 --> 00:29:35.340
that no, we can wait to replace
that filter at this point,

00:29:35.340 --> 00:29:40.300
or in the alternative scenario,
replace that filter right now,

00:29:40.300 --> 00:29:42.690
because we're going to have
to throw out some beer that's

00:29:42.690 --> 00:29:44.430
not going to pass muster.

00:29:44.430 --> 00:29:47.430
So it really is a
tremendous value

00:29:47.430 --> 00:29:49.050
that this is bringing to us.

00:29:49.050 --> 00:29:52.860
And we'll see how
it impacts things

00:29:52.860 --> 00:29:54.900
as we keep working with Pluto7.

00:29:54.900 --> 00:29:57.180
But we have really
high hopes and optimism

00:29:57.180 --> 00:30:01.012
for how this is going to affect
our business and our quality.

00:30:01.012 --> 00:30:02.160
MANJUNATH DEVADAS: Great.

00:30:02.160 --> 00:30:02.820
Thanks, Adam.

00:30:02.820 --> 00:30:07.090
So what does the underlying
architecture itself look like?

00:30:07.090 --> 00:30:10.907
It's pretty simple, and if
you're familiar with GCP,

00:30:10.907 --> 00:30:12.615
if you're already
using, you're like, ah.

00:30:12.615 --> 00:30:16.420
You recognize some
of these logos.

00:30:16.420 --> 00:30:21.000
So your ERP, where
the data comes from,

00:30:21.000 --> 00:30:25.740
and Dataflow which is
for traditional analytics

00:30:25.740 --> 00:30:26.290
architecture.

00:30:26.290 --> 00:30:28.200
It's the equivalent
of your ETL, but it's

00:30:28.200 --> 00:30:33.450
more with more power, and
scalability, and auto-scaling.

00:30:33.450 --> 00:30:36.270
The data, we brought
that into BigQuery.

00:30:36.270 --> 00:30:41.430
And we built a custom
ML model on GCML.

00:30:41.430 --> 00:30:44.675
And when I say custom ML model,
remember the three ML models

00:30:44.675 --> 00:30:46.800
I said, you know, one what
you saw this morning was

00:30:46.800 --> 00:30:48.329
auto ML, the one in between.

00:30:48.329 --> 00:30:49.870
The one on the left
is predetermined,

00:30:49.870 --> 00:30:53.270
more prebuilt model by Google,
where you don't build or train.

00:30:53.270 --> 00:30:56.010
And the one on the right
is the custom ML model.

00:30:56.010 --> 00:31:01.110
Well, for the small
section of the audience,

00:31:01.110 --> 00:31:04.950
in case you are relatively
new to machine learning model,

00:31:04.950 --> 00:31:08.290
you kind of want to use
machine learning model--

00:31:08.290 --> 00:31:10.800
I use this analogy whenever
I run customer workshops,

00:31:10.800 --> 00:31:14.055
is you kind of think as,
you just hired an intern,

00:31:14.055 --> 00:31:16.340
and you need to train
the intern on a job,

00:31:16.340 --> 00:31:18.340
then you check whether
the intern is doing well,

00:31:18.340 --> 00:31:20.310
and then you put
them in real job.

00:31:20.310 --> 00:31:23.660
It is the same process that
you go with machine learning.

00:31:23.660 --> 00:31:26.820
You kind of build the
model, you train the model,

00:31:26.820 --> 00:31:30.330
you give the data, and see
whether the model learned it.

00:31:30.330 --> 00:31:33.390
And then once you
have confidence

00:31:33.390 --> 00:31:35.880
in the model has learned it,
then you deploy the model

00:31:35.880 --> 00:31:38.030
and get it operationalizing--

00:31:38.030 --> 00:31:39.930
operationalize it.

00:31:39.930 --> 00:31:42.660
So we are kind of mimicking
the same process here,

00:31:42.660 --> 00:31:48.330
and the model continues to
learn based on how you define it

00:31:48.330 --> 00:31:49.780
and so on.

00:31:49.780 --> 00:31:52.470
So I'll kind of quickly
go into GCP console,

00:31:52.470 --> 00:31:55.920
just to show you what it
means to build a model

00:31:55.920 --> 00:31:57.840
and to deploy a
model, and so on.

00:31:57.840 --> 00:32:00.016
While we won't run
the model itself,

00:32:00.016 --> 00:32:01.890
it's something that you
have to kind of think

00:32:01.890 --> 00:32:06.270
that, OK, for this scenario
where we understood

00:32:06.270 --> 00:32:10.320
this process of data coming in,
we understood that, OK, it's

00:32:10.320 --> 00:32:11.910
a classification
problem of saying

00:32:11.910 --> 00:32:15.440
beer filter is good or bad
based on the data pattern.

00:32:15.440 --> 00:32:17.290
And we do this, and
we train the model

00:32:17.290 --> 00:32:19.350
so that it's making
good decisions.

00:32:19.350 --> 00:32:22.590
And training the model
is based on history.

00:32:22.590 --> 00:32:26.000
Over the last x
months or x years,

00:32:26.000 --> 00:32:28.820
when has filter
replacement happened?

00:32:28.820 --> 00:32:34.130
Whenever those filter
replacements happened,

00:32:34.130 --> 00:32:37.250
what was the pattern of the
data at that point in time?

00:32:37.250 --> 00:32:40.340
And the model is continuously
learning from that.

00:32:40.340 --> 00:32:44.105
The more data and the more
training there is, it's better,

00:32:44.105 --> 00:32:46.230
but it doesn't mean you
continuously keep training.

00:32:46.230 --> 00:32:48.050
There's an optimal
point up to which

00:32:48.050 --> 00:32:51.720
you want to train to get to your
level of accuracy, and so on.

00:32:51.720 --> 00:32:58.430
So with that, let me just
quickly go into GCP Console.

00:32:58.430 --> 00:33:03.930
So during this whole exercise
of understanding the business

00:33:03.930 --> 00:33:06.300
process, understanding
the use case,

00:33:06.300 --> 00:33:10.380
then we understand the data,
and from there we build a model.

00:33:10.380 --> 00:33:15.270
The model, once it's built,
is deployed in Google Cloud.

00:33:15.270 --> 00:33:20.910
So this is GCP Console,
in case anybody's new.

00:33:20.910 --> 00:33:23.220
So we would go and
built the model,

00:33:23.220 --> 00:33:25.450
and we would deploy
the model here.

00:33:25.450 --> 00:33:28.200
So the model itself is--

00:33:28.200 --> 00:33:30.000
at the core of it, you
want to think of it

00:33:30.000 --> 00:33:33.420
as a mathematical function that
has been data mined, right?

00:33:33.420 --> 00:33:36.640
Based on your data pattern,
it's a mathematical function,

00:33:36.640 --> 00:33:39.550
and that's hosted
on a TensorFlow.

00:33:39.550 --> 00:33:42.990
So now we leverage the
TensorFlow framework,

00:33:42.990 --> 00:33:47.030
and then you have deployed
the model, the version one.

00:33:47.030 --> 00:33:49.924
And this is a dummy model.

00:33:49.924 --> 00:33:51.840
For confidentiality
reasons, we can't show you

00:33:51.840 --> 00:33:54.840
the actual model, and so on.

00:33:54.840 --> 00:33:59.190
So you can put the model to
run repeatedly, and every day,

00:33:59.190 --> 00:34:01.020
or however often
you want the model

00:34:01.020 --> 00:34:02.925
to run, and train the model.

00:34:02.925 --> 00:34:06.060
To train the model,
so you schedule,

00:34:06.060 --> 00:34:08.159
you have the model
that's running,

00:34:08.159 --> 00:34:11.010
and then you have
a training job.

00:34:11.010 --> 00:34:13.020
So essentially, you
build the model,

00:34:13.020 --> 00:34:15.670
you deploy the model in the
cloud, and you train the model.

00:34:22.280 --> 00:34:26.090
So behind the scene, when I
said featuring engineering,

00:34:26.090 --> 00:34:29.830
and the column
selections, and so on,

00:34:29.830 --> 00:34:33.730
it's a combination of applying
your domain knowledge,

00:34:33.730 --> 00:34:37.179
and a combination of
applying data sciences,

00:34:37.179 --> 00:34:39.670
and understanding some of
the practicalities of how

00:34:39.670 --> 00:34:41.050
your model itself--

00:34:41.050 --> 00:34:43.179
what you want the model to do.

00:34:43.179 --> 00:34:44.929
So based on all
those things, you

00:34:44.929 --> 00:34:47.800
select your columns or features.

00:34:47.800 --> 00:34:49.929
That's what we have
it in the left.

00:34:49.929 --> 00:34:52.060
This is part of the model code.

00:34:52.060 --> 00:34:55.530
And then on the right is, which
deep learning model do you

00:34:55.530 --> 00:34:59.570
want to apply based
on your use case--

00:34:59.570 --> 00:35:02.450
it was a regression, in this
case, a classification--

00:35:02.450 --> 00:35:04.541
and which model works best?

00:35:04.541 --> 00:35:06.040
And there are
different factors that

00:35:06.040 --> 00:35:09.970
go into which model to select.

00:35:09.970 --> 00:35:13.080
There was a session earlier
today by Prashant Dhingra.

00:35:13.080 --> 00:35:15.190
He's probably around
in the audience.

00:35:15.190 --> 00:35:16.430
He's over there.

00:35:16.430 --> 00:35:21.390
And you can always ask him more
about which models to select.

00:35:21.390 --> 00:35:25.630
He's from Google, and has a lot
of experience in not only data

00:35:25.630 --> 00:35:29.820
mining, which is the right
model for the data scenario--

00:35:29.820 --> 00:35:34.420
the business scenario and the
kind of data that you have.

00:35:34.420 --> 00:35:36.370
It also comes with
a lot of experience

00:35:36.370 --> 00:35:41.080
that Google brings in by solving
not only internal Google's

00:35:41.080 --> 00:35:42.240
own problems--

00:35:42.240 --> 00:35:44.620
internal operational problems
where the machine learning

00:35:44.620 --> 00:35:46.580
has been applied.

00:35:46.580 --> 00:35:49.630
There are thousands
or tens of thousands

00:35:49.630 --> 00:35:52.030
of machine learning models
Google internal uses

00:35:52.030 --> 00:35:53.740
to run their own business.

00:35:53.740 --> 00:35:55.840
Along with that, the
industry experience

00:35:55.840 --> 00:35:58.600
solving for some of
the largest companies.

00:35:58.600 --> 00:36:00.710
Today you saw Target
in the keynote, right?

00:36:00.710 --> 00:36:02.380
So there are many,
many companies

00:36:02.380 --> 00:36:04.180
Google is continuously solving.

00:36:04.180 --> 00:36:08.210
All those learnings are going
into these deep learning models

00:36:08.210 --> 00:36:10.540
under the using
TensorFlow, and when

00:36:10.540 --> 00:36:13.740
you use some of these models
recommended by Google,

00:36:13.740 --> 00:36:16.270
there is a lot of intelligence
going behind it as well, so

00:36:16.270 --> 00:36:18.580
that some of the feature
engineering and all

00:36:18.580 --> 00:36:21.220
this burden that you
put on a person building

00:36:21.220 --> 00:36:24.910
on machine learning
models, those intelligence

00:36:24.910 --> 00:36:26.690
are part of it as well.

00:36:26.690 --> 00:36:29.620
So there are various sessions
at Google Next which go deeper

00:36:29.620 --> 00:36:31.645
into these models,
and also during Q&amp;A

00:36:31.645 --> 00:36:33.520
if there are a few things
that we can answer,

00:36:33.520 --> 00:36:35.840
we'll do that as well.

00:36:35.840 --> 00:36:37.780
One of the common
questions that I get

00:36:37.780 --> 00:36:39.700
asked when we are running
workshops, and proof

00:36:39.700 --> 00:36:41.200
of concepts, and
production rollouts

00:36:41.200 --> 00:36:45.460
is, very early on people
ask which model is the best

00:36:45.460 --> 00:36:48.980
model for what I want to solve.

00:36:48.980 --> 00:36:52.100
So we have to kind of take
them a few steps back.

00:36:52.100 --> 00:36:53.890
Do you even need a
machine learning model?

00:36:53.890 --> 00:36:56.054
Let's figure that out first.

00:36:56.054 --> 00:36:58.470
Half the time, people don't
need a machine learning model.

00:36:58.470 --> 00:37:00.386
They think they need a
machine learning model.

00:37:00.386 --> 00:37:01.870
They don't need it.

00:37:01.870 --> 00:37:03.630
The second thing
is, OK, even if you

00:37:03.630 --> 00:37:05.970
need a machine learning model,
do you have enough data--

00:37:05.970 --> 00:37:07.690
the right kind of data?

00:37:07.690 --> 00:37:11.130
Or even if the data is there,
for you to prepare the data,

00:37:11.130 --> 00:37:13.460
the whole ROI for
that is not justified.

00:37:13.460 --> 00:37:16.290
And so let's go through
some of these basics.

00:37:16.290 --> 00:37:18.007
Google strictly follows
these disciplines

00:37:18.007 --> 00:37:19.090
that when you don't need--

00:37:19.090 --> 00:37:22.080
and in fact, when I first
attended a Google machine

00:37:22.080 --> 00:37:25.350
learning training, if you don't
need a machine learning model,

00:37:25.350 --> 00:37:27.570
don't build one,
because you're going

00:37:27.570 --> 00:37:30.630
to just spend money and realize
that there are better ways

00:37:30.630 --> 00:37:31.180
to do it.

00:37:31.180 --> 00:37:35.790
But when you realize that you
have a right use case and right

00:37:35.790 --> 00:37:38.190
dataset, the power
of machine learning

00:37:38.190 --> 00:37:41.550
will be obvious by itself.

00:37:41.550 --> 00:37:46.410
So taking this a little
forward, the whole exercise

00:37:46.410 --> 00:37:48.960
was done in, like, six weeks.

00:37:48.960 --> 00:37:55.400
taking accuracy from 60%
to close to 92% accuracy.

00:37:55.400 --> 00:37:57.240
And based on the
region and zone,

00:37:57.240 --> 00:37:59.410
we do $1 million
savings, and so on.

00:37:59.410 --> 00:38:04.700
Now it's one experiment done
in one part of AB InBev.

00:38:04.700 --> 00:38:07.020
Now like this, there are
about 15-odd experiments

00:38:07.020 --> 00:38:09.660
or 20-odd experiments
AB InBev is considering.

00:38:09.660 --> 00:38:11.520
It's not so much
just the significance

00:38:11.520 --> 00:38:13.120
of what came out
of this project.

00:38:13.120 --> 00:38:16.590
It's rethinking how
you run your business.

00:38:16.590 --> 00:38:19.120
Now looking at your
manufacturing floor and saying,

00:38:19.120 --> 00:38:22.165
what else can I do with
this now that I'm on GCP,

00:38:22.165 --> 00:38:23.040
and I'm on the cloud.

00:38:23.040 --> 00:38:25.350
And I understand how
a machine learning

00:38:25.350 --> 00:38:30.060
model can be built mimicking
human decision making.

00:38:30.060 --> 00:38:32.562
So again, it's not
about replacing humans,

00:38:32.562 --> 00:38:33.770
it's about augmenting humans.

00:38:33.770 --> 00:38:36.350
We are not going to replace
every human decision making

00:38:36.350 --> 00:38:38.600
capability into an ML model.

00:38:38.600 --> 00:38:40.160
It doesn't work that way.

00:38:40.160 --> 00:38:42.000
Human brain is
incredibly complex.

00:38:42.000 --> 00:38:44.160
You can't mimic every
decision making,

00:38:44.160 --> 00:38:46.010
and those decisions
are not like--

00:38:46.010 --> 00:38:49.500
you cannot combine that
into one, holistic set.

00:38:49.500 --> 00:38:52.730
That are many smaller
decisions humans make,

00:38:52.730 --> 00:38:55.420
and there's an overarching
decision that's made,

00:38:55.420 --> 00:38:58.250
which kind of works
in coordination.

00:38:58.250 --> 00:39:02.140
So beyond building
models like this,

00:39:02.140 --> 00:39:04.810
it's also getting
stakeholder alignment

00:39:04.810 --> 00:39:07.330
and realizing that
you need more data.

00:39:07.330 --> 00:39:10.810
These are critical things that
become obvious to our customers

00:39:10.810 --> 00:39:13.170
as we build these models.

00:39:13.170 --> 00:39:14.710
Do you want to add
anything, Adam?

00:39:14.710 --> 00:39:15.793
ADAM SPUNBERG: Yeah, I do.

00:39:15.793 --> 00:39:17.950
So the stakeholder
alignment, this

00:39:17.950 --> 00:39:22.150
is an interesting question,
and obviously not every company

00:39:22.150 --> 00:39:24.520
has the same view of innovation.

00:39:24.520 --> 00:39:28.880
And within a company, there
are all kinds of apples.

00:39:28.880 --> 00:39:32.870
So the important thing--
and I'll be honest,

00:39:32.870 --> 00:39:34.600
this is something
that I've learned

00:39:34.600 --> 00:39:37.480
is just incredibly
valuable in navigating

00:39:37.480 --> 00:39:39.200
any kind of big
corporate culture--

00:39:39.200 --> 00:39:42.700
is you have to get buy-in
from the right people

00:39:42.700 --> 00:39:46.820
early on, because if you
start working on something

00:39:46.820 --> 00:39:49.570
and it's very exciting
maybe to the local team,

00:39:49.570 --> 00:39:53.160
maybe the brewmaster is really
into it, maybe people on site

00:39:53.160 --> 00:39:55.520
and even people in the
innovation program.

00:39:55.520 --> 00:39:58.720
But if you don't have those
higher stakeholders really

00:39:58.720 --> 00:40:00.700
supporting this,
you're going to run

00:40:00.700 --> 00:40:02.740
into obstacles along the way.

00:40:02.740 --> 00:40:05.680
And the way to
overcome that is, you

00:40:05.680 --> 00:40:07.840
have to make that
case early, and you

00:40:07.840 --> 00:40:10.090
have to make sure
what you're doing

00:40:10.090 --> 00:40:13.720
gets visibility
and is understood.

00:40:13.720 --> 00:40:17.490
And I think this is a
challenge for all of us.

00:40:17.490 --> 00:40:20.560
To some people machine
learning is a scary concept,

00:40:20.560 --> 00:40:25.450
or something that's just
beyond their realm of interest

00:40:25.450 --> 00:40:26.560
right now.

00:40:26.560 --> 00:40:28.480
And you can't
force that, but you

00:40:28.480 --> 00:40:31.990
have to find a way to slowly
bring about that understanding

00:40:31.990 --> 00:40:33.200
and evolution.

00:40:33.200 --> 00:40:36.970
So that's where I think it's
really effective when you can

00:40:36.970 --> 00:40:40.180
work collaboratively-- and
this is where Manju and I have

00:40:40.180 --> 00:40:41.590
really found some
common ground--

00:40:41.590 --> 00:40:44.470
is we both saw the
value in this project--

00:40:44.470 --> 00:40:46.000
obviously from two
different sides,

00:40:46.000 --> 00:40:48.460
but really we were
a team on this.

00:40:48.460 --> 00:40:51.400
We said, OK, what
can we do to make

00:40:51.400 --> 00:40:54.070
this compelling to
the right people?

00:40:54.070 --> 00:40:56.680
And we're still winning
some people over,

00:40:56.680 --> 00:40:58.750
but we got enough
people on board

00:40:58.750 --> 00:41:00.350
to really understand this.

00:41:00.350 --> 00:41:02.470
So for those of you
out there in companies

00:41:02.470 --> 00:41:05.560
that are looking to
pursue this track,

00:41:05.560 --> 00:41:08.590
I would say definitely
think about that aspect,

00:41:08.590 --> 00:41:11.590
because the impulse is
just go, how exciting,

00:41:11.590 --> 00:41:13.000
just run with this.

00:41:13.000 --> 00:41:15.850
But you have to think
bigger picture if you really

00:41:15.850 --> 00:41:18.910
want to implement an
impactful change that

00:41:18.910 --> 00:41:21.700
goes beyond the local aspects.

00:41:21.700 --> 00:41:23.250
MANJUNATH DEVADAS:
One key element

00:41:23.250 --> 00:41:26.840
I would like to highlight
is that ML is a journey.

00:41:26.840 --> 00:41:30.820
You start off with a project,
and you will comprehend first

00:41:30.820 --> 00:41:32.800
why this is powerful,
and so on, in a business

00:41:32.800 --> 00:41:35.230
context for our use case.

00:41:35.230 --> 00:41:38.120
Then it's an effort to kind
of bring everybody else

00:41:38.120 --> 00:41:40.130
along with you in the journey.

00:41:40.130 --> 00:41:43.580
And you have to assume that
some portion of the organization

00:41:43.580 --> 00:41:46.610
will not align with this
for various interests.

00:41:46.610 --> 00:41:49.170
Either they just don't want to
deal with the new technology,

00:41:49.170 --> 00:41:52.780
or it appears too complex,
it's not reliable--

00:41:52.780 --> 00:41:54.440
whatever their
reasons are, there

00:41:54.440 --> 00:41:56.930
is a certain amount of
awareness education,

00:41:56.930 --> 00:41:59.520
and some folks don't
want to go with it.

00:41:59.520 --> 00:42:02.060
So you have to realize
what battles you're picking

00:42:02.060 --> 00:42:05.630
and keep progressing, and
realize that this is a journey.

00:42:05.630 --> 00:42:07.130
You're in it, your
company is in it,

00:42:07.130 --> 00:42:09.410
the whole ecosystem is in it.

00:42:09.410 --> 00:42:14.450
So just to summarize from a
prediction quality standpoint,

00:42:14.450 --> 00:42:18.830
with a 92% accuracy, taking
about six months of data,

00:42:18.830 --> 00:42:26.506
and getting to the
right kind of values

00:42:26.506 --> 00:42:28.130
so that the business
has the confidence

00:42:28.130 --> 00:42:31.640
that, yes, if we do this there
is a certain amount of money

00:42:31.640 --> 00:42:32.822
that's getting saved.

00:42:32.822 --> 00:42:34.280
Not only money
getting saved, there

00:42:34.280 --> 00:42:37.310
is improvement in
our production--

00:42:37.310 --> 00:42:40.070
reduction in our production
downtime, and so on.

00:42:40.070 --> 00:42:44.540
So I had to go in and present
to the C-level executives

00:42:44.540 --> 00:42:46.790
on how it was
done, and why there

00:42:46.790 --> 00:42:50.240
was so much of a difference, and
how it is manual versus machine

00:42:50.240 --> 00:42:51.720
learning model, and so on.

00:42:51.720 --> 00:42:53.490
So it's pretty
impressive, too, for a--

00:42:53.490 --> 00:42:56.980
I believe you're a 100-plus
year old company, right?

00:42:56.980 --> 00:43:00.080
ADAM SPUNBERG: Yeah, the first
breweries in Anheuser-Busch,

00:43:00.080 --> 00:43:01.280
anyway, were in St. Louis.

00:43:01.280 --> 00:43:04.514
And obviously there's been
mergers and acquisitions

00:43:04.514 --> 00:43:05.930
and all kinds of
stuff since then.

00:43:05.930 --> 00:43:09.350
But you know, this isn't a
technology or a product that

00:43:09.350 --> 00:43:12.470
was invented
yesterday, but it keeps

00:43:12.470 --> 00:43:14.687
evolving with time just
like anything else.

00:43:14.687 --> 00:43:16.520
MANJUNATH DEVADAS: And
one of the executives

00:43:16.520 --> 00:43:20.780
messaging that they want to
make it an AI-based brewery,

00:43:20.780 --> 00:43:23.820
I mean that's pretty amazing.

00:43:23.820 --> 00:43:28.170
So in summary, there's a
filter replacement problem,

00:43:28.170 --> 00:43:30.640
and the outcomes
we just discussed,

00:43:30.640 --> 00:43:35.110
which is replacing the filter at
the right time driving millions

00:43:35.110 --> 00:43:37.850
of savings by region,
zone, and so on.

00:43:37.850 --> 00:43:42.100
[MUSIC PLAYING]

